# The voice host's entry in the launcher's app table, merged into the launcher
# config `RobotCpu` renders (`cogs/robot.clk`) so that one supervisor starts and
# stops every process a unit runs.
#
# One message of simplelaunch's `AppConfig`. Hand-written for the reason
# `driver/motord_launch.textproto` is: the renderer only knows about Clockwork
# processes, and the host is not one -- it is a plain process outside the
# composition, on the far side of the socket seam, which is where everything
# that can be malformed is refused before it reaches a reader that dies on it.
#
# The executable is resolved against the launcher's working directory, which is
# the payload root -- so this is where `tools/build-motion.sh` stages the host
# binary. Every path below is relative to that same directory.
#
# The host's default `--config` is `host/host_params.textproto` and the payload
# stages the operator's own file there, so the edge half needs no argument: the
# per-unit file is a push into RAM rather than an edit to this table, and the
# tree carries only `host/host_params.example.textproto`, which is staged by
# nothing.
#
# `--speech-config` is named unconditionally and the file it names is not in
# this tree and never will be: a speech configuration holds a site's STT and TTS
# endpoints, its bus server and token, and this unit's link keys, so every copy
# of it is somebody's own and travels as a push into RAM.
# `tools/build-motion.sh` stages whatever `REACHY_SPEECH_CONFIG` names -- by
# default the gitignored `host/speech.toml` of the working tree -- and a payload
# built without one simply has no file at this path. That is why the argument
# can be unconditional: a host whose speech configuration is absent runs its
# edge half and says so on its own console, where a launcher app that exited
# would have taken the session's narration with it.
#
# Named `voice_host` rather than `host`: the launcher spells each app's console
# `<name>_<run>.log` and this repo says "host" for the workstation half of the
# bench -- `make motion-host-run`, `cogs/robot_host.clk`, `hostcpu.textproto` --
# so a bare `host_0.log` in a unit's log directory reads as the wrong machine's
# output.
#
# Merged into the production config only. The harness twin
# (`cogs:robotcpu_harness.textproto`) leaves it out, because `--run` starts
# `reachy_ask` -- and two processes both binding the narration port 7410 and
# both addressing scripts to 7409 in one run is exactly the collision the twin
# exists to prevent.
app {
  name: "voice_host"
  executable: "reachy_host"
  args: "--speech-config"
  args: "host/speech.toml"
}
